# Support Vector Machines (SVM): Generalization, Kernel Methods, and Noise Robustness

**Course:** Machine Learning and Neural Networks for Neuroscience (`27504-01`) — Bar-Ilan University[cite: 5]  
**Lecturer:** Dr. Zvi Roth[cite: 5]  
**Assignment:** Homework 2 — Support Vector Machines (SVM)

---

## Overview

This repository contains the Python implementation, visualization scripts, and empirical analysis for **Homework 2**, which investigates how linear and kernel-based Support Vector Machines behave under varying geometric structures, Gaussian data augmentations, training label corruption, and high-dimensional feature spaces.

All experiments use a fixed random seed (`seed=42`) to ensure full reproducibility across runs.

---

## Repository Structure

```text
├── data_generation.py          # Task 1: Synthetic dataset generators (Linear & Concentric Circular)
├── linear_vs_perceptron.py     # Task 2: Perceptron vs. Soft-Margin Linear SVM comparison
├── kernel_svm.py               # Task 3: Linear, RBF, and Polynomial (d=2, 3) Kernel SVMs
├── data_augmentation.py        # Task 4: Generalization analysis across k=2,000 Gaussian noise batches
├── label_noise.py              # Task 5: Training label shuffling (15%–100%) and soft-margin resilience
├── high_dimensionality.py      # Task 6: Scaling datasets and label-noise tests to d = 2, 5, 10, 15
├── bonus_threshold.py          # Bonus: Sweeping Gaussian noise SD to locate the 70% accuracy threshold
├── report/
│   └── HW2_SVM_Report.pdf      # Complete written report with plots, tables, and conclusions
├── requirements.txt            # Python dependencies
└── README.md
